  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe cold front that moved through South Texas Tuesday will return as \na warm front Thursday, bringing a low chance of showers Thursday \nafternoon to the southern Coastal Bend. The chance of showers and \nthunderstorms increases to a medium to high chance Thursday night \ninto Friday morning as another cold front moves through S TX. Rain \nchances are forecast to decrease as the cold front moves south of \nthe area. Models indicate a second surge of high pressure that will \nreinforce the cooler temperatures late Saturday into Sunday, and \nbring another round of showers and thunderstorms to S TX. However, \nmodels have been flip flopping with rain chances for the weekend, \nthus, confidence is low for this scenario to pan out. Models show \nrain chances decreasing Sunday night as drier air advects across the \nregion. \n\nAs for the temperatures, highs will be warmer with lower to mid 80s \non Thursday due to the warm front, but the next cold front on Friday \nwill bring max temperatures down to the 70s Friday and Saturday. The \nreinforcing surge late Saturday will bring highs in the 60s to \naround 70 for Sunday and Monday. Lows in the 50s to around 60 can be \nexpected by Saturday night with the second reinforcing surge of \nhigh pressure.\n\nThe chance of minor coastal flooding along gulf facing beaches will \nincrease through Friday and especially by the weekend. This is due \nto a strengthening northeast flow by Saturday which is conducive for \neckman forcing to push the water up to the dunes during high tide. \nIn addition, normal tide levels will also be on the increase as we \nhead toward a new moon. Latest P-ETSS run indicated that tide levels \nmay exceed the 2ft MSL threshold during times of high tide by Friday \nand into the weekend. The one thing missing will be long period \nswells. Swell periods are only expected to get up to 7 seconds. Will \nmonitor trends for any changes to the tide levels and swells.",
